From c9927e8af3da155a2fa73149e3f26f721c111cd9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Clasen <d.clasen@qvest-digital.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 00:20:25 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] ice: handle VIRTCHNL_OP_CONFIG_QUEUE_TC_MAP from VFs

DPDK's iavf traffic manager sends VIRTCHNL_OP_CONFIG_QUEUE_TC_MAP after
VIRTCHNL_OP_CONFIG_QUEUE_BW when it commits a queue rate limit, and
fails the commit when the PF refuses it. The in-tree PF accepts the
bandwidth message but has no handler for the TC mapping, so a VF cannot
commit a per-queue rate limit at all.

The handler, its validation and the message layout are taken from
Intel's out-of-tree ice driver (2.6.7 and 2.7.6 carry the same code),
with the message's one-element tail turned into a flexible array and a
_LEGACY_SIZEOF, the way the in-tree virtchnl.h declares its other
variable-length messages. struct ice_virtchnl_ops already declares
cfg_q_tc_map; this sets it in both op tables.

The response carries ICE_VF_VSI_ID, the VSI id the in-tree PF gives
the VF in VIRTCHNL_OP_GET_VF_RESOURCES.

Two things differ from the out-of-tree handler. A TC's priority bitmap
ORs every user priority mapped to it, as ice_vc_get_qos_caps() does,
where the original assigned and kept only the last one. And the VSI's
tc_info and the rings' dcb_tc change only after ice_update_vsi()
succeeds; the original changed them first and kept them when the
update failed. An admin queue failure is reported as such rather than
as out of memory.

Signed-off-by: Daniel Clasen <d.clasen@qvest-digital.com>
